              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Let "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "n _ { \\mathrm { { } _ { 1 } } } , n _ { \\mathrm { { } _ { 2 } } }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "n _ { 3 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " be three numbers."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.203,
            0.11,
            0.339,
            0.129
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_35_equation_1.jpg",
          "content": "S = n_{1} + n_{2} + n_{3}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.201,
            0.128,
            0.845,
            0.167
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "By dividing a number by 3, the only possible remainders are 0, 1 and 2."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.202,
            0.173,
            0.845,
            0.214
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Case I : Suppose the remainders are "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm { R } _ { 1 } , \\mathrm { R } _ { 2 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathtt { R _ { 3 } } ."
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " If "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm { R } _ { _ 1 } = 0"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm { R } _ { 2 } \\neq \\mathrm { R } _ { 3 } ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " then,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.203,
            0.214,
            0.578,
            0.233
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_35_equation_4.jpg",
          "content": "\\mathrm{R}_{1}+\\mathrm{R}_{2}+\\mathrm{R}_{3}=0+1+2=3 \\mathrm{or} 0+2+1=3"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.202,
            0.233,
            0.559,
            0.253
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "So, the Sum(S) will be divisible by 3."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.202,
            0.258,
            0.474,
            0.28
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Case II: Suppose "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "R_{1} = R_{2} = R_{3}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.202,
            0.284,
            0.545,
            0.307
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Then "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm{R}_{1}+\\mathrm{R}_{2}+\\mathrm{R}_{3}=3\\mathrm{R}_{1} \\text { or } 3\\mathrm{R}_{2} \\text { or } 3\\mathrm{R}_{3}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.201,
            0.31,
            0.847,
            0.35
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "So, the Sum(S) will be divisible by 3 if the sum of the remainders is 0, 3 or 6."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.168,
            0.359,
            0.848,
            0.453
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "14. Is the product of two consecutive integers always multiple of 2? Why? What about the product of three consecutive integers? Is it always a multiple of 6? Why or why not? What can you say about the product of 4 consecutive integers? What about the product of five consecutive integers?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.461,
            0.201,
            0.478
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ans."
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.216,
            0.478,
            0.621,
            0.497
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(i) Product of two consecutive integers—"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.22,
            0.497,
            0.845,
            0.558
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Yes, product of two consecutive integers is always a multiple of 2, because if one is odd then the other will be even and multiple of even is even."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.258,
            0.569,
            0.648,
            0.588
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Product of three consecutive integers—"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.256,
            0.589,
            0.846,
            0.65
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Yes, the product of three consecutive integers is always a multiple of 6. There is at least one multiple of 2 and one multiple of 3 in the product of three consecutive integers."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.257,
            0.66,
            0.609,
            0.679
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Product of 4 consecutive integers—"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.256,
            0.679,
            0.847,
            0.739
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The product will be multiple of 24 because at least one of them is a multiple of 2, one is a multiple of 3, and one is a multiple of 4."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
